{% icon id="battle-gear" size=32 /%}Same army, many targets? A virtual preset is a set of attack waves saved under a name. Any attack module of the same account can send it, so you write the waves once and use them on many pages.

Virtual presets live in GGE Command, not in the game. That is why they are called virtual. The page has no {% term id="plugin" %}plugin{% /term %} of its own, so it does not count toward your {% term id="attack-cap" %}attack cap{% /term %}.

{% screenshot id="virtual-presets" alt="The Virtual presets page for one account: the New preset button, a count of 1 of 30 presets, and a preset named Ranged first with a summary of its waves and the Edit, Duplicate and Delete buttons." /%}

## Make a preset {% #make %}

1. Open {% ui key="client:modules.virtual-presets.name" /%} in the sidebar and pick the account at the top. Presets belong to one account.
2. Choose {% ui key="client:presets.new" /%}.
3. Give the preset a name, such as "Towers, ranged first". Each name can be used once per account.
4. Write the waves in the wave editor, as described below.
5. Choose {% ui key="common:action.save" /%}.

An account keeps up to 30 presets. The page shows how many you have, and {% ui key="client:presets.new" /%} is locked when you reach 30.

## The wave editor {% #wave-editor %}

The same editor appears on the attack pages when **Army** is set to {% ui key="client:army.mode.waves" /%}.

{% screenshot id="army-editor" alt="The wave editor on an attack page: wave 1 open on Custom slots, with tool and troop slots for the left flank, the front and the right flank, and an Archer slot with an amount of 40." /%}

- Write 1 to 25 waves. A new plan starts with 4. Choose {% ui key="client:waves.add" /%} for another.
- The last wave is sent again for each wave after it, so the castle needs troops for those waves too.
- The game sends at most 4 waves at level 51 and above, plus any your {% term id="commander-game" %}commander{% /term %} adds. Waves past that are kept, but not sent.
- Each wave has slots for tools and troops on the left flank, the front and the right flank. Pick an item in a slot, then type how many.
- {% ui key="client:waves.copyRest" /%} copies a wave into every wave after it.
- {% ui key="client:waves.support.title" /%} has 3 slots for tools that go with the whole attack.
- {% ui key="client:waves.courtyard.title" /%} has 8 rows for the courtyard.

An amount above what a side can hold fills the side. The bot waits while the castle holds less than a slot needs.

Each wave can also come from an {% ui key="client:waves.kind.preset" /%}. The bot can't read in-game presets yet, so a module whose waves use one does not start. Use {% ui key="client:waves.kind.custom" /%} for now. {% status kind="planned" /%}

## Use a preset in a module {% #use %}

1. Open an attack module and pick the same account.
2. Set **Army** to {% ui key="client:army.mode.waves" /%}.
3. Under **Waves**, switch from {% ui key="client:waves.source.manual" /%} to {% ui key="client:waves.source.virtual" /%}, and pick the preset.
4. Save the module.

The module now sends the preset's waves. When you edit the preset, every module that sends it gets the change, with no need to save those modules again. {% ui key="client:waves.source.manage" /%} on the module page takes you here.

If you switch back to {% ui key="client:waves.source.manual" /%}, the waves you wrote on that page before come back.

## Duplicate a preset {% #duplicate %}

Choose {% ui key="client:presets.duplicate" /%} next to a preset. The copy gets the same waves and the name with "(copy)" at the end. Edit the copy to try a change without touching the preset your modules send.

## Delete a preset {% #delete %}

Choose {% ui key="client:presets.delete" /%} and confirm. This can't be undone.

You can't delete a preset that a module still sends. The page tells you which modules send it, and each preset in the list shows **Sent by** with those modules. Open each one, pick other waves there, and save. Then delete the preset.

{% aside type="note" id="why-in-use" %}
A module keeps no copy of the preset's waves. It reads them from the preset. If the preset were gone, the module would have nothing to send.
{% /aside %}

If a preset does go missing, for example after a delete in another browser tab, the module shows **This virtual preset was deleted. Pick another one.** and sends nothing until you pick other waves.

## Related errors {% #errors %}

- [VIRTUAL_PRESET_IN_USE](/errors/#virtual-preset-in-use): a module still sends the preset you tried to delete.
- [VIRTUAL_PRESET_LIMIT](/errors/#virtual-preset-limit): the account already has 30 presets.
- [VIRTUAL_PRESET_DUPLICATE](/errors/#virtual-preset-duplicate): the account already has a preset with that name.
- [VIRTUAL_PRESET_NOT_FOUND](/errors/#virtual-preset-not-found): the preset was deleted, maybe in another tab.
- [PLUGIN_OPTION_ARMY](/errors/#plugin-option-army): a module's army setting can't be read.
